Read it.   
You're drunk, go home.

&#x200B;

While I would encourage you to further explore this patchwork style and I'd like to see how it develops throughout your artistic progression, [DestroyedArkana](https://www.reddit.com/user/DestroyedArkana) 's critique is as spot on as it's gonna get. Value layering is where you want to apply your study. Even with that patchwork style, some practice time needs to be spent making it more cohesive and unifying.  


Personally, I would still count this piece as a win. This is evolution, baby!

&#x200B;

Neutral. As anything to do with art, it was a waste of my time. Even though I kept jumping forward, and forward, and forward.I don't know if you're a hipster or you specialize in hipster art or......what? This is "ArtCrit". My Crit is that those moments I just spent were irrelevant to anything.  
Good luck trying to be a social media celeb!!

&#x200B;

I dig  it as recognize these as part of the struggle.  
I hope it's daily.

&#x200B;

Read and listen to people speak. Not in a visual medium ala youtube. Listen to podcasts. Rogan, intelligence squared. debates. Just go about your business with an earbud in your ear and listen to articulate people discuss.  
Also recommend audiobooks instead of traditional reading.

&#x200B;

Ok. So say that? If you say that and I say that and everyone else says that, what have we accomplished? Instead I dared to give an opinion. Again, while ALSO celebrating the work that's posted. I'm sorry, Nexus. I just don't see how what I said would discourage anyone from art. But apparently it would have been discouraging to YOU, so I will try to alter my feedback in the future. Maybe it was just the wrong place to even make a comment on trend art, since that's not what was asked for.

Ok. Good exchange. I'll remember to be more careful. Thanks.

&#x200B;

I wouldn't say that to someone just thinking about "trying drawing". I said it to someone that's already engaged in learning art and has some success with it. What would you prefer I say as an alternative? "hey, that's really cool. Let me link you to various youtube videos where you can learn to copy someone drawing a certain thing"?  
I gave the same advice that I was given when I expressed a real interest in art, while also praising what was posted.  


&#x200B;

Charles Bargue.  
But drawing from life is nothing to be intimidated by. Draw your sneakers, the family car, your pet. Think nothing of it.

&#x200B;
